<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Hud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1039–1110</span> · Also known as Hudids, Hudid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hudid dynasty (Banu Hud) ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> from 1039 to 1110. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-ibn-hud">Sulayman ibn Hud al-Mustain</a>, it reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir">Ahmad al-Muqtadir</a>, who annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aljaferia">Aljafería</a> palace, and it employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> in the 1080s. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> took Zaragoza in 1110 the family held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rueda-de-jalon">Rueda de Jalón</a>, and a descendant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud">Ibn Hud</a>, led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banu-hud.jpg" alt="Banu Hud" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ecelan · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Aljafer%C3%ADa_-_Palacio_taifa_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
